            "title": "Market Drawdown Vs. Downturn: Watch The Fundamentals",
            "description": "<html><body><p data-eci=\"true\"><figure class=\"getty-figure\" data-type=\"getty-image\"><picture> <img alt=\"Businesswoman using a tablet computer analyzing stock exchange market crypto trading decreasing chart data fall down loss, desperate about losing money of crisis, recession, inflation.Economic crisis\" data-id=\"1425118031\" data-type=\"getty-image\" fetchpriority=\"high\" height=\"1024\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 132px), (max-width: 1199px) calc(100vw - 666px), (max-width: 1307px) calc(100vw - 708px), 600px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1425118031/image_1425118031.jpg?io=getty-c-w630\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1425118031/image_1425118031.jpg?io=getty-c-w1536 1536w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1425118031/image_1425118031.jpg?io=getty-c-w1280 1280w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1425118031/image_1425118031.jpg?io=getty-c-w1080 1080w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1425118031/image_1425118031.jpg?io=getty-c-w750 750w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1425118031/image_1425118031.jpg?io=getty-c-w640 640w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1425118031/image_1425118031.jpg?io=getty-c-w480 480w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1425118031/image_1425118031.jpg?io=getty-c-w320 320w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1425118031/image_1425118031.jpg?io=getty-c-w240 240w\" width=\"1536\"/> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"> </p> <p class=\"item-credits\">pcess609/iStock via Getty Images</p></figcaption></figure></p>  <p><em>By Brian Levitt, Chief Global Market Strategist and Head of Strategy &amp; Insights</em></p> <p>I’m often asked when the next market drawdown will occur. An easy answer is soon enough. After all, 5-10% corrections happen in most years.</p></body></html>",
